## How Interfaces Solve the Diamond Problem in Java

The **diamond problem** occurs when a class inherits the same method from **two parent types**, causing ambiguity.

Java solves this using **interfaces** and **explicit method implementation rules**.

---

## Diamond Structure

```
        A
       / \
      B   C
       \ /
        D
```

If both `B` and `C` contain the same method, class `D` must decide **which implementation to use**.

---

# Case 1 — Interfaces Without Default Methods

Interfaces only declare methods (before Java 8).

Example:

```java
interface A {
    void show();
}

interface B {
    void show();
}
```

Implementation:

```java
class C implements A, B {

    public void show() {
        System.out.println("Implementation in C");
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
public class App {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        C obj = new C();
        obj.show();

    }

}
```

Output

```
Implementation in C
```

No ambiguity because **class must implement the method**.

---

# Case 2 — Java 8 Default Method Conflict

Java 8 introduced **default methods**.

Example:

```java
interface A {

    default void show() {
        System.out.println("A show");
    }

}

interface B {

    default void show() {
        System.out.println("B show");
    }

}
```

Implementation must resolve conflict:

```java
class C implements A, B {

    public void show() {
        A.super.show();
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
public class App {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        C obj = new C();
        obj.show();

    }

}
```

Output

```
A show
```

---

# Why Interfaces Solve Diamond Problem

Java forces **explicit resolution**.

Rules:

```
1. Class method > Interface default method
2. Child class must override conflicting default methods
3. Specific interface can be called using Interface.super.method()
```

---

# Example Showing Rule 1

```java
class A {

    void show() {
        System.out.println("Class A");
    }

}

interface B {

    default void show() {
        System.out.println("Interface B");
    }

}

class C extends A implements B { }
```

Usage:

```java
C obj = new C();
obj.show();
```

Output

```
Class A
```

Because:

```
Class method > Interface default method
```

---

# One-line Interview Answer

**Interfaces solve the diamond problem by forcing the implementing class to explicitly override conflicting methods, eliminating ambiguity between multiple parent implementations.**

