## Difference Between Factory and Abstract Factory (Java)

Both are **creational design patterns** used for **object creation**, but they differ in **scope and complexity**.

---

## 1. Factory Pattern (Factory Method)

Factory Pattern creates **one type of object based on input**.

Basic idea:

```text id="yupctk"
Factory → Creates single product
```

### Example

```java id="hs2s3n"
interface Vehicle {

    void start();

}
```

Implementations:

```java id="0j1ov3"
class Car implements Vehicle {

    public void start(){
        System.out.println("Car started");
    }

}
```

```java id="ql4n1l"
class Bike implements Vehicle {

    public void start(){
        System.out.println("Bike started");
    }

}
```

Factory class:

```java id="86hl9d"
class VehicleFactory {

    public static Vehicle getVehicle(String type){

        if(type.equals("car"))
            return new Car();

        if(type.equals("bike"))
            return new Bike();

        return null;
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java id="zvazra"
Vehicle v = VehicleFactory.getVehicle("car");
v.start();
```

---

## 2. Abstract Factory Pattern

Abstract Factory creates **families of related objects**.

Basic idea:

```text id="9ntxjs"
Abstract Factory
      ↓
Multiple Factories
      ↓
Create related objects
```

### Example

Interfaces:

```java id="jpujyg"
interface Button {
    void paint();
}

interface Checkbox {
    void paint();
}
```

Implementations:

```java id="rct7il"
class WindowsButton implements Button {

    public void paint(){
        System.out.println("Windows Button");
    }

}
```

```java id="nxj6zo"
class MacButton implements Button {

    public void paint(){
        System.out.println("Mac Button");
    }

}
```

Abstract factory:

```java id="4u45y7"
interface GUIFactory {

    Button createButton();
    Checkbox createCheckbox();

}
```

Concrete factory:

```java id="cduj03"
class WindowsFactory implements GUIFactory {

    public Button createButton(){
        return new WindowsButton();
    }

    public Checkbox createCheckbox(){
        return new WindowsCheckbox();
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java id="ztlflh"
GUIFactory factory = new WindowsFactory();

Button button = factory.createButton();

button.paint();
```

---

## Key Differences

| Feature | Factory Pattern | Abstract Factory |
|---|---|---|
| Purpose | Create single object | Create families of objects |
| Complexity | Simple | More complex |
| Number of products | One | Multiple related products |
| Structure | One factory class | Factory of factories |

---

## Real Examples in Java

Factory:

```java id="78htq4"
Calendar.getInstance()
```

Abstract Factory:

```java id="3w3d4q"
javax.xml.parsers.DocumentBuilderFactory
```

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**Factory Pattern creates a single type of object based on input, while Abstract Factory provides an interface to create families of related objects without specifying their concrete classes.**

## Method Overloading vs Method Overriding (Java)

Both provide **polymorphism**, but they work differently.

---

## 1. Method Overloading

**Method Overloading = same method name with different parameters in the same class.**

Rules:

```text
method name → same
parameters → different
class → same
```

Example:

```java
class Calculator {

    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }

    int add(int a, int b, int c) {
        return a + b + c;
    }

    double add(double a, double b) {
        return a + b;
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
public class App {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Calculator c = new Calculator();

        System.out.println(c.add(5,10));
        System.out.println(c.add(5,10,15));
        System.out.println(c.add(5.5,2.5));

    }

}
```

Output

```
15
30
8.0
```

Resolved at:

```
Compile Time
```

So overloading = **compile-time polymorphism**.

---

## 2. Method Overriding

**Method Overriding = child class provides new implementation of parent method.**

Rules:

```text
method name → same
parameters → same
class → parent + child
```

Example:

```java
class Animal {

    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Animal makes sound");
    }

}
```

Child class:

```java
class Dog extends Animal {

    @Override
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }

}
```

Usage:

```java
public class App {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Animal a = new Dog();

        a.sound();

    }

}
```

Output

```
Dog barks
```

Resolved at:

```
Runtime
```

So overriding = **runtime polymorphism**.

---

## Key Differences

| Feature | Overloading | Overriding |
|---|---|---|
| Method name | Same | Same |
| Parameters | Different | Same |
| Class | Same class | Parent + Child |
| Return type | Can differ | Must be same / covariant |
| Binding | Compile time | Runtime |
| Annotation | Not required | `@Override` recommended |

---

## Visual Example

```
Overloading

add(int,int)
add(int,int,int)
add(double,double)
```

```
Overriding

Animal → sound()
Dog    → sound()
```

---

## One-line Interview Answer

**Method overloading allows multiple methods with the same name but different parameters in the same class, while method overriding allows a child class to provide a new implementation of a parent class method with the same signature.**

If the **`finally` block throws an exception**, it **overrides any exception thrown in the `try` or `catch` block**.

So the exception from `finally` becomes the **actual exception propagated to the caller**.

---

## Example

```java
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        try {

            int x = 10 / 0;   // ArithmeticException

        } finally {

            throw new RuntimeException("Exception from finally");

        }

    }
}
```

Output

```
Exception in thread "main"
java.lang.RuntimeException: Exception from finally
```

Notice:

```
ArithmeticException is lost
```

because the **finally exception overrides it**.

---

## Example with try–catch–finally

```java
public class Demo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        try {

            int x = 10 / 0;

        } catch(Exception e) {

            System.out.println("Catch block");

        } finally {

            throw new RuntimeException("Finally exception");

        }

    }
}
```

Output

```
Catch block
Exception in thread "main"
java.lang.RuntimeException: Finally exception
```

The exception in `finally` still propagates.

---

## Internal Execution Flow

```
try block throws exception
        ↓
finally executes
        ↓
finally throws new exception
        ↓
new exception overrides previous one
```

---

## Example Showing Lost Exception

```java
try {
    throw new Exception("Try Exception");
} finally {
    throw new Exception("Finally Exception");
}
```

Output

```
Finally Exception
```

The **try exception is suppressed**.

---

## Special Case — Try-With-Resources

With **try-with-resources**, Java keeps suppressed exceptions.

Example:

```java
try(Resource r = new Resource()){
    throw new Exception("Main Exception");
}
```

If `close()` throws exception, JVM keeps:

```
Main Exception
Suppressed: close() exception
```

You can access suppressed exceptions:

```java
exception.getSuppressed();
```

---

## Best Practice

Avoid throwing exceptions inside `finally`.

Better approach:

```java
finally {
    try {
        resource.close();
    } catch(Exception e){
        log(e);
    }
}
```

---

✅ **One-line interview answer**

If the **finally block throws an exception, it overrides any exception thrown in the try or catch block and becomes the final exception propagated by the JVM.**

## Lazy Initialization (Java)

**Lazy Initialization** is a technique where **an object is created only when it is needed instead of at application startup**.

This helps **reduce memory usage and improve startup performance**.

