## **How Builder improves immutability**

The **Builder Pattern** helps create **fully initialized objects once** and then **locks their state** (no setters, only `final` fields).

---

## **1. Immutable Class using Builder**

```java id="im1"
public final class User {

    private final String name;
    private final int age;
    private final String email;

    private User(Builder b){
        this.name = b.name;
        this.age = b.age;
        this.email = b.email;
    }

    public String getName(){ return name; }
    public int getAge(){ return age; }
    public String getEmail(){ return email; }

    public static class Builder {

        private String name;
        private int age;
        private String email;

        public Builder name(String name){
            this.name = name;
            return this;
        }

        public Builder age(int age){
            this.age = age;
            return this;
        }

        public Builder email(String email){
            this.email = email;
            return this;
        }

        public User build(){
            return new User(this); // object created once
        }
    }
}
```

---

## **2. Usage**

```java id="im2"
User u = new User.Builder()
        .name("Prem")
        .age(30)
        .email("p@gmail.com")
        .build();
```

👉 After `build()` → object cannot change

---

## **3. Why this makes object immutable**

### ✅ **A. All fields are `final`**

```java
private final String name;
```

👉 Value set once → cannot be reassigned

---

### ✅ **B. No setters**

```java
// no setName(), setAge()
```

👉 No modification after creation

---

### ✅ **C. Object created in one step**

```java
return new User(this);
```

👉 No partial/inconsistent state

---

### ✅ **D. Builder is mutable, object is immutable**

```java id="im3"
User.Builder b = new User.Builder();
b.name("Prem");
b.age(30);

// still mutable here

User u = b.build(); // now immutable
```

---

### ✅ **E. Validation before creation**

```java id="im4"
public User build(){
    if(name == null) throw new IllegalStateException("Name required");
    return new User(this);
}
```

👉 Prevent invalid objects

---

## **4. Thread Safety Benefit**

Immutable object = automatically thread-safe

```java id="im5"
User u = new User.Builder().name("Prem").build();

// safe to share across threads
```

---

## **5. Without Builder (problem ❌)**

```java id="im6"
class User {
    String name;
    int age;
}

// mutable
user.name = "A";
user.name = "B"; // changed anytime ❌
```

---

## ⚡ **Key Idea**

```text
Builder = controlled construction
Immutable Object = fixed state after creation
```

---

## 🎯 **Short Interview Answer**

> The Builder Pattern improves immutability by allowing all fields to be set during object construction and making the final object immutable using final fields and no setters. The builder itself is mutable, but the constructed object is created once in a fully initialized state and cannot be modified afterward.

## **Factory Method vs Abstract Factory (clear + code)**

---

## **1. Factory Method → creates ONE product**

👉 Subclass decides **which object to create**

```java id="fmvaf1"
interface Notification {
    void send();
}

class EmailNotification implements Notification {
    public void send(){ System.out.println("Email"); }
}

abstract class NotificationFactory {

    abstract Notification create(); // factory method

    public void notifyUser(){
        Notification n = create();
        n.send();
    }
}

class EmailFactory extends NotificationFactory {
    Notification create(){ return new EmailNotification(); }
}
```

Usage

```java id="fmvaf2"
NotificationFactory factory = new EmailFactory();
factory.notifyUser();
```

---

## **2. Abstract Factory → creates FAMILY of products**

👉 One factory creates **multiple related objects**

```java id="afvsfm1"
interface Button { void render(); }
interface Checkbox { void check(); }

class WindowsButton implements Button {
    public void render(){ System.out.println("Win Button"); }
}

class WindowsCheckbox implements Checkbox {
    public void check(){ System.out.println("Win Checkbox"); }
}
```

Factory

```java id="afvsfm2"
interface GUIFactory {
    Button createButton();
    Checkbox createCheckbox();
}

class WindowsFactory implements GUIFactory {

    public Button createButton(){
        return new WindowsButton();
    }

    public Checkbox createCheckbox(){
        return new WindowsCheckbox();
    }
}
```

Usage

```java id="afvsfm3"
GUIFactory factory = new WindowsFactory();

Button b = factory.createButton();
Checkbox c = factory.createCheckbox();
```

---

## **3. Key Differences**

| Feature | Factory Method | Abstract Factory |
|---|---|---|
| Purpose | Create one object | Create family of objects |
| Control | Subclass decides | Factory decides family |
| Complexity | Simple | More complex |
| Example | EmailNotification | Windows UI (Button + Checkbox) |
| Extensibility | Add new product via subclass | Add new family via factory |

---

## **4. Mental Model (IMPORTANT)**

```text
Factory Method
    → "Which object?"

Abstract Factory
    → "Which family of objects?"
```

---

## **5. Relationship**

👉 Abstract Factory often uses **Factory Method internally**

---

## **6. When to Use**

### Factory Method
✔ Single object creation  
✔ Need flexibility in subclass  

---

### Abstract Factory
✔ Multiple related objects  
✔ Need consistency (same family)  
✔ Example: UI themes, DB drivers, cloud providers  

---

## **7. Real Usage**

Frameworks like **entity["software","Spring Framework","Java framework"]**

- BeanFactory → Factory Method  
- ApplicationContext → Abstract Factory style  

