## **Abstract Factory Pattern**

The **Abstract Factory Pattern** is a **creational design pattern** that:

> **Provides an interface for creating families of related objects without specifying their concrete classes.**

👉 Instead of creating **one object**, it creates a **group (family) of related objects**.

---

## **Problem (without Abstract Factory ❌)**

```java id="af1"
class Application {

    Button button = new WindowsButton();
    Checkbox checkbox = new MacCheckbox(); // ❌ mismatch (Windows + Mac)

}
```

👉 UI inconsistency  
👉 Tight coupling  
👉 Hard to switch themes (Windows/Mac)

---

## **Solution (Abstract Factory ✅)**

### **1. Abstract Products**

```java id="af2"
interface Button {
    void render();
}

interface Checkbox {
    void check();
}
```

---

### **2. Concrete Products**

```java id="af3"
class WindowsButton implements Button {
    public void render(){ System.out.println("Windows Button"); }
}

class MacButton implements Button {
    public void render(){ System.out.println("Mac Button"); }
}

class WindowsCheckbox implements Checkbox {
    public void check(){ System.out.println("Windows Checkbox"); }
}

class MacCheckbox implements Checkbox {
    public void check(){ System.out.println("Mac Checkbox"); }
}
```

---

### **3. Abstract Factory**

```java id="af4"
interface GUIFactory {

    Button createButton();
    Checkbox createCheckbox();
}
```

---

### **4. Concrete Factories (Family creators)**

```java id="af5"
class WindowsFactory implements GUIFactory {

    public Button createButton(){
        return new WindowsButton();
    }

    public Checkbox createCheckbox(){
        return new WindowsCheckbox();
    }
}

class MacFactory implements GUIFactory {

    public Button createButton(){
        return new MacButton();
    }

    public Checkbox createCheckbox(){
        return new MacCheckbox();
    }
}
```

---

### **5. Usage**

```java id="af6"
class Application {

    private Button button;
    private Checkbox checkbox;

    public Application(GUIFactory factory){
        button = factory.createButton();
        checkbox = factory.createCheckbox();
    }

    public void renderUI(){
        button.render();
        checkbox.check();
    }
}
```

```java id="af7"
GUIFactory factory = new WindowsFactory(); // or MacFactory
Application app = new Application(factory);
app.renderUI();
```

---

## **Key Idea**

👉 **Create related objects together (same family)**  
👉 Avoid mixing incompatible objects  

---

## **Benefits**

✔ Ensures consistency (same family objects)  
✔ Follows Open/Closed Principle  
✔ Loose coupling  
✔ Easy to switch families (Windows → Mac)

---

## **Difference from Factory Method**

| Feature | Factory Method | Abstract Factory |
|---|---|---|
| Creates | One product | Family of products |
| Example | Button | Button + Checkbox |
| Complexity | Low | Higher |

---

## **Real-World Usage**

Frameworks like **entity["software","Spring Framework","Java framework"]** use this idea for:

- Creating related beans  
- Environment-based configurations  

---

## 🎯 **Short Interview Answer**

> The Abstract Factory Pattern provides an interface to create families of related or dependent objects without specifying their concrete classes. It ensures consistency among related objects and allows switching between different product families easily.

**Design Patterns** are **reusable solutions to commonly occurring problems in software design**. They are **proven templates or best practices** that developers use to structure code in a scalable, maintainable, and reusable way.

They are **not finished code**, but rather **guidelines on how to organize classes and objects** to solve a recurring design problem.

Example: Instead of creating objects directly using `new`, we may use a **Factory Pattern** to centralize object creation.

```java
interface Notification {
    void send();
}

class EmailNotification implements Notification {
    public void send() {
        System.out.println("Sending Email");
    }
}

class SMSNotification implements Notification {
    public void send() {
        System.out.println("Sending SMS");
    }
}

class NotificationFactory {

    public static Notification create(String type) {

        if(type.equals("email"))
            return new EmailNotification();

        if(type.equals("sms"))
            return new SMSNotification();

        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid type");
    }
}
```

Usage

```java
Notification notification = NotificationFactory.create("email");
notification.send();
```

---

**Why Design Patterns Are Important**

**1. Reusable Solutions**

Instead of solving the same problem repeatedly, patterns provide **tested solutions**.

**2. Improves Code Maintainability**

Patterns create **clear architecture and separation of concerns**.

Example

```java
// Without pattern
if(type.equals("paypal")) { ... }
else if(type.equals("stripe")) { ... }
else if(type.equals("razorpay")) { ... }

// With Strategy Pattern
PaymentStrategy strategy = new StripePayment();
strategy.pay();
```

---

**3. Promotes Loose Coupling**

Classes depend on **interfaces instead of concrete implementations**.

```java
