## **Factory Method vs Simple Factory**

Both create objects, but the **control of creation** is different.

---

## **1. Simple Factory (Not GoF)**

👉 **Single class decides which object to create**

```java id="sf1"
interface Notification {
    void send();
}

class EmailNotification implements Notification {
    public void send(){ System.out.println("Email"); }
}

class SmsNotification implements Notification {
    public void send(){ System.out.println("SMS"); }
}

class NotificationFactory {

    public static Notification create(String type){

        if(type.equals("EMAIL")) return new EmailNotification();
        if(type.equals("SMS"))   return new SmsNotification();

        throw new RuntimeException();
    }
}
```

Usage

```java id="sf2"
Notification n = NotificationFactory.create("EMAIL");
n.send();
```

### ❌ Problems
- Uses `if/else` or `switch`  
- Violates **Open/Closed Principle**  
- Adding new type → modify factory  

---

## **2. Factory Method (GoF Pattern)**

👉 **Subclasses decide which object to create**

```java id="fm1"
interface Notification {
    void send();
}

class EmailNotification implements Notification {
    public void send(){ System.out.println("Email"); }
}

class SmsNotification implements Notification {
    public void send(){ System.out.println("SMS"); }
}

abstract class NotificationFactory {

    abstract Notification create();

    public void send(){
        Notification n = create();
        n.send();
    }
}
```

Concrete factories

```java id="fm2"
class EmailFactory extends NotificationFactory {
    Notification create(){ return new EmailNotification(); }
}

class SmsFactory extends NotificationFactory {
    Notification create(){ return new SmsNotification(); }
}
```

Usage

```java id="fm3"
NotificationFactory factory = new EmailFactory();
factory.send();
```

---

## **3. Key Differences**

| Feature | Simple Factory | Factory Method |
|---|---|---|
| Type | Utility pattern | GoF design pattern |
| Creation logic | Centralized (one class) | Delegated to subclasses |
| Extensibility | ❌ Modify existing code | ✅ Add new class |
| OCP (Open/Closed) | ❌ Violated | ✅ Followed |
| Complexity | Simple | More flexible |

---

## **4. When to Use**

### Use **Simple Factory**
✔ Few object types  
✔ Stable logic  
✔ Quick solution  

---

### Use **Factory Method**
✔ Many types  
✔ Frequent changes  
✔ Need scalability  
✔ Framework-level design  

---

## ⚡ **Real Insight (Industry)**

- Small projects → Simple Factory  
- Large systems / frameworks (like entity["software","Spring Framework","Java framework"]) → Factory Method  

---

