Here are **industry-standard ways to implement a thread-safe Singleton in Java** (pick based on your use case):

---

## **1. Double-Checked Locking (most common)**

```java
public class Singleton {

    private static volatile Singleton instance;

    private Singleton() {}

    public static Singleton getInstance() {

        if (instance == null) {                 // 1st check
            synchronized (Singleton.class) {
                if (instance == null) {         // 2nd check
                    instance = new Singleton();
                }
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

✔ Lazy initialization  
✔ Thread-safe  
✔ Good performance (minimal synchronization)

---

## **2. Bill Pugh (Initialization-on-demand holder) ✅ BEST**

```java
public class Singleton {

    private Singleton() {}

    private static class Holder {
        private static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();
    }

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        return Holder.INSTANCE;
    }
}
```

✔ Thread-safe (JVM class loading)  
✔ Lazy  
✔ No synchronization overhead  
👉 **Most recommended in real projects**

---

## **3. Enum Singleton (safest)**

```java
public enum Singleton {
    INSTANCE;

    public void doSomething() {
        System.out.println("Hello");
    }
}
```

✔ Thread-safe  
✔ Prevents reflection attack  
✔ Handles serialization automatically  
👉 Used in critical systems

---

## **4. Eager Initialization (simple but not lazy)**

```java
public class Singleton {

    private static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();

    private Singleton() {}

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        return INSTANCE;
    }
}
```

✔ Thread-safe  
❌ Not lazy (created even if unused)

---

## ❌ **Wrong (Not Thread-Safe)**

```java
public class Singleton {

    private static Singleton instance;

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new Singleton(); // race condition ❌
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
```

---

## ⚡ **When to use which**

| Approach | Use Case |
|---|---|
| Bill Pugh | Default choice |
| Enum | Security-critical / serialization |
| Double-checked | Fine control / legacy systems |
| Eager | Simple + always needed |

---

## 🎯 **Short Interview Answer**

> A thread-safe Singleton in Java can be implemented using techniques like double-checked locking with a volatile instance, the Bill Pugh inner static class approach, or an enum. The Bill Pugh method is generally preferred because it is lazy, thread-safe, and avoids synchronization overhead.

## **Prototype Pattern**

The **Prototype Pattern** is a **creational design pattern** that:

> **Creates new objects by copying (cloning) an existing object instead of creating from scratch.**

👉 Useful when object creation is **expensive or complex**.

---

## **1. Problem (Heavy Object Creation ❌)**

```java id="pp1"
class Document {

    String content;

    public Document(String content){
        // simulate heavy operation
        try { Thread.sleep(1000); } catch(Exception e){}
        this.content = content;
    }
}
```

```java id="pp2"
// slow: every time new object
Document d1 = new Document("Report");
Document d2 = new Document("Report");
```

👉 Expensive initialization repeated

---

## **2. Solution (Prototype Pattern ✅)**

```java id="pp3"
class Document implements Cloneable {

    String content;

    public Document(String content){
        this.content = content;
    }

    @Override
    protected Document clone() {
        try {
            return (Document) super.clone(); // shallow copy
        } catch (CloneNotSupportedException e) {
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
    }
}
```

---

## **Usage**

```java id="pp4"
Document original = new Document("Report");

Document copy1 = original.clone();
Document copy2 = original.clone();
```

👉 Fast cloning  
👉 Avoid expensive creation  

---

## **3. Shallow vs Deep Copy**

### **Shallow Copy (default)**

```java id="pp5"
return (Document) super.clone();
```

👉 Copies references (not objects inside)

---

### **Deep Copy (manual)**

```java id="pp6"
class Address implements Cloneable {
    String city;

    protected Address clone() throws CloneNotSupportedException {
        return (Address) super.clone();
    }
}

class User implements Cloneable {

    Address address;

    protected User clone() throws CloneNotSupportedException {
        User u = (User) super.clone();
        u.address = address.clone(); // deep copy
        return u;
    }
}
```

👉 Copies nested objects also

---

## **4. When to Use**

✔ Object creation is expensive  
✔ Need many similar objects  
✔ Avoid complex constructors  
✔ Need copy of existing state  

---

## **5. Real-World Usage**

- Object templates  
- Caching objects  
- Game development (copy characters)  
- Spring uses cloning internally in some cases (**entity["software","Spring Framework","Java framework"]**)  

