## Default Methods (Java 8)

👉 **Definition (crisp)**  
Default methods are methods inside interfaces with a **body (implementation)** using the `default` keyword.

---

## Basic Example

```java id="dm1"
interface A {

    default void show() {
        System.out.println("Default method");
    }
}
```

---

## Usage

```java id="dm2"
class Test implements A {}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Test t = new Test();
        t.show(); // Default method
    }
}
```

---

## Why Introduced? 🔥

👉 To **add new methods in interfaces without breaking existing implementations**

---

## Example (Backward Compatibility)

```java id="dm3"
interface A {
    void run();

    default void log() {
        System.out.println("Logging...");
    }
}
```

👉 Old classes don’t need to implement `log()`

---

## Override Default Method

```java id="dm4"
class Test implements A {

    public void run() {}

    @Override
    public void log() {
        System.out.println("Custom log");
    }
}
```

---

## Multiple Interface Conflict 🔥

```java id="dm5"
interface A {
    default void show() { System.out.println("A"); }
}

interface B {
    default void show() { System.out.println("B"); }
}

class Test implements A, B {
    public void show() {
        A.super.show(); // resolve conflict
    }
}
```

---

## Key Points

- Defined using `default` keyword  
- Have implementation  
- Can be overridden  
- Helps in backward compatibility  

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 Default methods are **methods in interfaces with a body, allowing interfaces to evolve without breaking existing implementations**.

