## Lazy Evaluation (Stream API)

👉 **Definition (crisp)**  
Lazy evaluation means **intermediate operations are NOT executed until a terminal operation is called**.

---

## Example

```java id="le1"
List<Integer> list = Arrays.asList(1,2,3,4,5);

list.stream()
    .filter(x -> {
        System.out.println("Filtering: " + x);
        return x > 2;
    });
// ❌ No output (no terminal operation)
```

---

## Execution Starts Only with Terminal Operation

```java id="le2"
list.stream()
    .filter(x -> {
        System.out.println("Filtering: " + x);
        return x > 2;
    })
    .forEach(System.out::println); // ✅ triggers execution
```

---

## Pipeline Behavior (Important 🔥)

```java id="le3"
list.stream()
    .filter(x -> {
        System.out.println("Filter: " + x);
        return x > 2;
    })
    .map(x -> {
        System.out.println("Map: " + x);
        return x * 2;
    })
    .forEach(System.out::println);
```

👉 Output shows **one-by-one processing**, not full pass

---

## Output Flow 🔥

```
Filter: 1
Filter: 2
Filter: 3
Map: 3
6
Filter: 4
Map: 4
8
...
```

👉 Each element goes through **entire pipeline step-by-step**

---

## Benefits

- Better performance  
- Avoids unnecessary computation  
- Supports short-circuiting (`findFirst`, `anyMatch`)  

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 Lazy evaluation means **stream operations are executed only when a terminal operation is invoked, and elements are processed one at a time through the pipeline**.

