## Lambda vs Method Reference (Java 8)

---

## 1. Basic Idea

```java id="lm1"
// Lambda → defines logic
x -> x * x

// Method Reference → uses existing method
Math::abs
```

---

## 2. Syntax

```java id="lm2"
// Lambda
list.forEach(x -> System.out.println(x));

// Method Reference
list.forEach(System.out::println);
```

---

## 3. Code Size / Readability

```java id="lm3"
// Lambda → more flexible but slightly verbose
(a, b) -> a.length() - b.length()

// Method Reference → shorter & cleaner
String::compareTo
```

---

## 4. Logic vs Reuse 🔥

```java id="lm4"
// Lambda → custom logic
n -> n % 2 == 0

// Method Reference → existing method reuse
Integer::parseInt
```

---

## 5. Flexibility

```java id="lm5"
// Lambda → can write complex logic
x -> {
    int y = x * 2;
    return y + 1;
};

// Method Reference ❌ cannot do this
```

---

## 6. When to Use

```java id="lm6"
// Use Lambda → when logic is new/custom
list.stream().filter(x -> x > 10);

// Use Method Reference → when method already exists
list.forEach(System.out::println);
```

---

## 7. Types Support

```java id="lm7"
// Lambda → any functional interface

// Method Reference → only if matching method exists
```

---

## Summary 🔥

```java id="lm8"
// Lambda = (params) -> logic
// Method Ref = Class::method (reuse existing logic)
```

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 Lambda defines **inline behavior**, while method reference is a **shortcut to reuse an existing method using `::`**.

