Yes ✅ — **Lambda expressions can throw exceptions**, but there are rules 👇

---

## 1. Unchecked Exceptions (Runtime) ✅

👉 No restriction

```java id="u1a2b3"
List<String> list = Arrays.asList("A", "B", "C");

list.forEach(s -> {
    if (s.equals("B")) {
        throw new RuntimeException("Error");
    }
});
```

---

## 2. Checked Exceptions ⚠️ (Important)

👉 Depends on **functional interface method signature**

---

### ❌ Not Allowed (Directly)

```java id="c4d5e6"
list.forEach(s -> {
    throw new IOException(); // ❌ compile-time error
});
```

👉 `Consumer.accept()` does NOT declare `throws`

---

### ✅ Solution 1: Try-Catch inside Lambda

```java id="f7g8h9"
list.forEach(s -> {
    try {
        if (s.equals("A")) {
            throw new IOException();
        }
    } catch (IOException e) {
        e.printStackTrace();
    }
});
```

---

### ✅ Solution 2: Wrap Exception (Sneaky Throw Style)

```java id="i1j2k3"
list.forEach(s -> {
    try {
        risky();
    } catch (IOException e) {
        throw new RuntimeException(e);
    }
});

static void risky() throws IOException {}
```

---

### ✅ Solution 3: Custom Functional Interface

```java id="l4m5n6"
@FunctionalInterface
interface MyFunc {
    void run() throws Exception;
}

MyFunc f = () -> {
    throw new Exception("Checked");
};
```

---

## 3. Real Interview Trick 🔥

```java id="o7p8q9"
Function<String, Integer> f = s -> {
    try {
        return Integer.parseInt(s);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        return 0;
    }
};
```

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 Lambda expressions can throw exceptions, but **checked exceptions are only allowed if the functional interface method declares them**; otherwise they must be handled inside the lambda.

