## `orElse()` vs `orElseGet()` (Java 8)

---

## 1. Basic Difference

```java id="oe1"
// orElse → takes value
opt.orElse("Default");

// orElseGet → takes Supplier
opt.orElseGet(() -> "Default");
```

---

## 2. Execution (Important 🔥)

```java id="oe2"
// orElse → ALWAYS executes
opt.orElse(getDefault());

// orElseGet → executes ONLY if value is absent
opt.orElseGet(() -> getDefault());
```

---

## 3. Proof Example 🔥

```java id="oe3"
static String getDefault() {
    System.out.println("Executing...");
    return "Default";
}

Optional<String> opt = Optional.of("Java");

// orElse
opt.orElse(getDefault()); 
// prints: Executing ❗

// orElseGet
opt.orElseGet(() -> getDefault()); 
// prints nothing ✅
```

---

## 4. Performance

```java id="oe4"
// orElse → may waste computation ❌
// orElseGet → efficient (lazy) ✅
```

---

## 5. Use Case

```java id="oe5"
// simple value
opt.orElse("Default");

// heavy computation / DB call
opt.orElseGet(() -> fetchFromDB());
```

---

## Summary 🔥

```java id="oe6"
// orElse    → eager execution
// orElseGet → lazy execution
```

---

## One-line Answer 💡

👉 `orElse()` always evaluates the default value, while `orElseGet()` **evaluates it lazily only when the Optional is empty**, making it more efficient.

