Here are **industry-level ways** to find **all pairs with a given sum** 👇

---

### 1. Using HashSet (Most Important 🔥)
```java id="ps1k9x"
import java.util.*;

public class PairSum {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {2, 7, 11, 15, -2, 9};
        int target = 9;

        Set<Integer> set = new HashSet<>();

        for (int num : arr) {
            int complement = target - num;

            if (set.contains(complement)) {
                System.out.println(complement + " + " + num);
            }

            set.add(num);
        }
    }
}
```

---

### 2. Using HashMap (Handles duplicates 🔥)
```java id="ps2p7m"
import java.util.*;

public class PairSum {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1, 5, 7, -1, 5};
        int target = 6;

        Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

        for (int num : arr) {
            int complement = target - num;

            if (map.getOrDefault(complement, 0) > 0) {
                System.out.println(complement + " + " + num);
                map.put(complement, map.get(complement) - 1);
            } else {
                map.put(num, map.getOrDefault(num, 0) + 1);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

---

### 3. Two Pointer (Sorted Array 🔥)
```java id="ps3v2z"
import java.util.*;

public class PairSum {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6};
        int target = 7;

        int left = 0, right = arr.length - 1;

        while (left < right) {
            int sum = arr[left] + arr[right];

            if (sum == target) {
                System.out.println(arr[left] + " + " + arr[right]);
                left++;
                right--;
            } else if (sum < target) {
                left++;
            } else {
                right--;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

---

### 4. Brute Force (Simple but O(n²))
```java id="ps4d8q"
public class PairSum {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {2, 7, 11, 15};
        int target = 9;

        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            for (int j = i + 1; j < arr.length; j++) {
                if (arr[i] + arr[j] == target) {
                    System.out.println(arr[i] + " + " + arr[j]);
                }
            }
        }
    }
}
```

---

### 5. Return Indices (Leetcode style)
```java id="ps5n3w"
import java.util.*;

public class PairSum {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {2, 7, 11, 15};
        int target = 9;

        Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

        for (int i = 0; i < arr.length; i++) {
            int complement = target - arr[i];

            if (map.containsKey(complement)) {
                System.out.println(map.get(complement) + ", " + i);
                return;
            }

            map.put(arr[i], i);
        }
    }
}
```

---

### 6. Unique Pairs Only (Avoid duplicates)
```java id="ps6c2l"
import java.util.*;

public class PairSum {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {1, 5, 7, -1, 5};
        int target = 6;

        Set<Integer> seen = new HashSet<>();
        Set<String> output = new HashSet<>();

        for (int num : arr) {
            int complement = target - num;

            if (seen.contains(complement)) {
                int a = Math.min(num, complement);
                int b = Math.max(num, complement);
                output.add(a + "," + b);
            }

            seen.add(num);
        }

        for (String pair : output) {
            System.out.println(pair);
        }
    }
}
```

