## 🔹 Compile-time access (normal Java)

```java
class User {
    public String greet(String name) {
        return "Hello " + name;
    }
}

User user = new User();
String res = user.greet("Prem");   // ✅ compile-time checked
```

### ✔️ Characteristics
- Type-safe
- Fast (JVM optimizations like inlining)
- Errors caught at compile time
- Direct method/field access

---

## 🔹 Reflection (runtime access)

```java
Class<?> clazz = Class.forName("User");

Object obj = clazz.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();

Method method = clazz.getDeclaredMethod("greet", String.class);
String res = (String) method.invoke(obj, "Prem"); // runtime call
```

### ✔️ Characteristics
- No compile-time type checking
- Slower (no inlining, dynamic dispatch)
- Works even if class not known at compile time
- Can access private members

---

## 🔥 Side-by-side comparison

```java
// Compile-time
User u = new User();
u.greet("Prem");

// Reflection
Class<?> c = User.class;
Object o = c.getDeclaredConstructor().newInstance();
Method m = c.getDeclaredMethod("greet", String.class);
m.invoke(o, "Prem");
```

---

## 🔑 Key Differences

| Feature | Compile-time Access | Reflection |
|--------|-------------------|-----------|
| Binding time | Compile-time | Runtime |
| Performance | Fast ⚡ | Slower 🐢 |
| Type safety | Yes ✅ | No ❌ |
| Access private members | No ❌ | Yes (setAccessible) |
| IDE support | Full (auto-complete) | None |
| Use case | Business logic | Frameworks, dynamic systems |

---

## 🔥 Real-world insight

```java
// Compile-time (your code)
userService.save(user);

// Reflection (framework)
for (Field f : obj.getClass().getDeclaredFields()) {
    if (f.isAnnotationPresent(Autowired.class)) {
        f.setAccessible(true);
        f.set(obj, dependency);
    }
}
```

👉 **You write compile-time code**  
👉 **Frameworks use reflection under the hood**

---

## 🧠 One-liner

👉 *Compile-time access is static, fast, and safe; Reflection is dynamic, flexible, but slower and less safe.*

