## ⚡ Use Case: **API Response Caching (Most Practical)**

👉 Scenario:  
Tumhara Node.js / Spring Boot API DB se data fetch karta hai (slow 🐢)  
→ Redis cache use karke fast response ⚡

---

## 🧠 Flow

```
Client → API → Redis (check)
                  ↓
             (miss) → DB → Redis set → Response
                  ↓
             (hit) → Response (fast)
```

---

## 🚀 Node.js Example (Express + Redis)

### 📦 Install
```bash
npm install express redis
```

---

### 🔥 redisClient.js
```js
const redis = require("redis");

const client = redis.createClient({
  url: "redis://localhost:6379"
});

client.on("error", (err) => console.log("Redis Error:", err));

(async () => {
  await client.connect();
})();

module.exports = client;
```

---

### 🔥 app.js
```js
const express = require("express");
const redisClient = require("./redisClient");

const app = express();

// fake DB
const getUsersFromDB = async () => {
  console.log("Fetching from DB...");
  return [
    { id: 1, name: "Prem" },
    { id: 2, name: "John" }
  ];
};

// GET API with Redis cache
app.get("/users", async (req, res) => {
  const cacheKey = "users";

  // 1. check cache
  const cached = await redisClient.get(cacheKey);

  if (cached) {
    return res.json({
      source: "redis",
      data: JSON.parse(cached)
    });
  }

  // 2. fetch from DB
  const users = await getUsersFromDB();

  // 3. store in Redis (TTL = 60 sec)
  await redisClient.setEx(cacheKey, 60, JSON.stringify(users));

  res.json({
    source: "db",
    data: users
  });
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log("Server running on port 3000"));
```

