Below is a **clean, production-style Java implementation** for your rule (bidirectional + propagation for synonyms & antonyms).  
Minimal explanation, mostly code as you prefer 👇

---

### ✅ Model

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Word {
    private final String text;
    private final Set<String> synonyms = new HashSet<>();
    private final Set<String> antonyms = new HashSet<>();

    public Word(String text) {
        this.text = text.toLowerCase();
    }

    public String getText() { return text; }
    public Set<String> getSynonyms() { return synonyms; }
    public Set<String> getAntonyms() { return antonyms; }
}
```

---

### ✅ Repository (In-Memory)

```java
import java.util.*;

public class WordRepository {

    private final Map<String, Word> store = new HashMap<>();

    public Word getOrCreate(String word) {
        return store.computeIfAbsent(word.toLowerCase(), Word::new);
    }

    public Word get(String word) {
        return store.get(word.toLowerCase());
    }

    public Collection<Word> findAll() {
        return store.values();
    }
}
```

---

### ✅ Service (Core Logic 🔥)

```java
import java.util.*;

public class ThesaurusService {

    private final WordRepository repo;

    public ThesaurusService(WordRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }

    // -------------------------------
    // ADD SYNONYMS
    // -------------------------------
    public void addSynonyms(String base, List<String> synonyms) {
        Word baseWord = repo.getOrCreate(base);

        for (String syn : synonyms) {
            Word synWord = repo.getOrCreate(syn);

            // A <-> C (bidirectional)
            baseWord.getSynonyms().add(synWord.getText());
            synWord.getSynonyms().add(baseWord.getText());

            // propagate synonyms among themselves (C <-> D <-> E)
            for (String other : synonyms) {
                if (!other.equalsIgnoreCase(syn)) {
                    synWord.getSynonyms().add(other.toLowerCase());
                }
            }
        }

        // also add all synonyms to base
        baseWord.getSynonyms().addAll(
            synonyms.stream().map(String::toLowerCase).toList()
        );
    }

    // -------------------------------
    // ADD ANTONYMS (WITH PROPAGATION)
    // -------------------------------
    public void addAntonym(String wordA, String wordB) {

        Word A = repo.getOrCreate(wordA);
        Word B = repo.getOrCreate(wordB);

        // direct A <-> B
        A.getAntonyms().add(B.getText());
        B.getAntonyms().add(A.getText());

        // RULE:
        // if A has synonyms C,D,E
        // then B should have A,C,D,E as antonyms

        Set<String> aSyns = A.getSynonyms();

        for (String syn : aSyns) {
            Word synWord = repo.getOrCreate(syn);

            // B -> (A, C, D, E)
            B.getAntonyms().add(synWord.getText());

            // (C, D, E) -> A
            synWord.getAntonyms().add(A.getText());

            // also bidirectional consistency
            synWord.getAntonyms().add(B.getText());
        }

        // also add A's synonyms to B
        B.getAntonyms().addAll(aSyns);

        // reverse propagation (optional but strong consistency)
        Set<String> bSyns = B.getSynonyms();
        for (String syn : bSyns) {
            Word synWord = repo.getOrCreate(syn);

            A.getAntonyms().add(synWord.getText());
            synWord.getAntonyms().add(A.getText());
        }
    }

    // -------------------------------
    // VIEW
    // -------------------------------
    public void printWord(String word) {
        Word w = repo.get(word);
        if (w == null) return;

        System.out.println("Word: " + w.getText());
        System.out.println("Synonyms: " + w.getSynonyms());
        System.out.println("Antonyms: " + w.getAntonyms());
        System.out.println("---------------------");
    }
}
```

---

### ✅ Usage Example

```java
import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        WordRepository repo = new WordRepository();
        ThesaurusService service = new ThesaurusService(repo);

        // A = happy
        // C,D,E = joyful, cheerful, glad
        service.addSynonyms("happy", Arrays.asList("joyful", "cheerful", "glad"));

        // B = sad
        service.addAntonym("happy", "sad");

        service.printWord("happy");
        service.printWord("joyful");
        service.printWord("sad");
    }
}
```

