### 🔹 String vs StringBuffer (Java)

| Feature | String | StringBuffer |
|--------|--------|-------------|
| Mutability | ❌ Immutable | ✅ Mutable |
| Thread Safety | ❌ Not thread-safe | ✅ Thread-safe (synchronized) |
| Performance | Slow (new object each change) | Faster (in-place change) |
| Memory | More (creates new objects) | Less (modifies same object) |
| Use Case | Read-only data | Frequent modifications |

---

### 🔹 Code Examples

#### 1. String (Immutable)
```java
public class TestString {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String s = "Prem";
        s.concat(" Kumar"); // new object created, ignored

        System.out.println(s); // Prem

        s = s.concat(" Kumar");
        System.out.println(s); // Prem Kumar
    }
}
```

---

#### 2. StringBuffer (Mutable)
```java
public class TestStringBuffer {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer("Prem");

        sb.append(" Kumar"); // modifies same object
        System.out.println(sb); // Prem Kumar

        sb.reverse();
        System.out.println(sb); // ramuK merP
    }
}
```

---

### 🔹 Thread Safety Demo
```java
class BufferTest extends Thread {
    static StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer("Hello");

    public void run() {
        sb.append(" World");
        System.out.println(sb);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        BufferTest t1 = new BufferTest();
        BufferTest t2 = new BufferTest();

        t1.start();
        t2.start();
    }
}
```

---

### 🔹 Performance Difference
```java
public class PerformanceTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        long start = System.currentTimeMillis();

        String s = "";
        for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {
            s += "a";
        }

        long end = System.currentTimeMillis();
        System.out.println("String Time: " + (end - start));

        start = System.currentTimeMillis();

        StringBuffer sb = new StringBuffer();
        for (int i = 0; i < 10000; i++) {
            sb.append("a");
        }

        end = System.currentTimeMillis();
        System.out.println("StringBuffer Time: " + (end - start));
    }
}
```

---

### 🔹 Quick Rule (Interview Ready)
```text
String → Immutable → Safe but slow  
StringBuffer → Mutable → Thread-safe but slower than StringBuilder  
StringBuilder → Best performance (not thread-safe)
```

